      "topic": {
        "text": "Execution flow of a Java for loop",
        "refs": ["Q0002", "Q0003", "Q0004"]
      },
      "explanation_text": {
        "text": "When Java encounters a for loop, it first executes the initialization statement (e.g., initializing the counter variable to zero). Then it evaluates the loop condition; if true, it executes the loop body. After the body, it performs the increment step, then re-evaluates the condition. This repeats until the condition is false, at which point control moves outside the loop.",
        "refs": ["Q0002", "Q0003", "Q0004"]
      }
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    {
      "fact_type": null,
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "A Java for loop consists of three parts inside parentheses: initialization of a counter variable, a boolean condition that determines loop continuation, and an increment/decrement operation.",
        "refs": ["Q0000", "Q0001"]
      }
    },
    {
      "fact_type": null,
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "Common counter variable names for loops are i, j, and k.",
        "refs": ["Q0001"]
      }
    },
    {
      "fact_type": null,
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "If the loop body contains a single statement, braces are optional; for multiple statements, a code block with braces is required.",
        "refs": ["Q0002"]
      }
    },
    {
      "fact_type": null,
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "To execute a loop exactly five times, you can initialize the counter to 0 and use the condition i < 5, or initialize to 1 and use i <= 5.",
        "refs": ["Q0004"]
      }
    }
  ],
